Title: A Mad Tea-Party
Credit: adapted from
Author: Lewis Carroll's Alice's Adventures in Wonderland (1865)
Draft date: Sample for Wit
Notes: A short screenplay sample. Carroll's text is in the public domain.

FADE IN:

EXT. MARCH HARE'S HOUSE - GARDEN - LATE AFTERNOON

A house with chimneys shaped like ears and a roof thatched with fur. Under a tree, a long table is set for tea, with far too many cups.

At one end sit the MARCH HARE and the HATTER, a battered top hat on his head, a price ticket still tucked into the band: "In this style 10/6".

Between them, the DORMOUSE is fast asleep. The other two rest their elbows on it, like a cushion, and talk over its head.

ALICE (7) comes through the gate. The Hare and the Hatter see her at once.

MARCH HARE
No room! No room!

HATTER
(waving her off)
No room!

ALICE
(indignant)
There's *plenty* of room!

She sits down in a large armchair at one end of the table.

MARCH HARE
(encouragingly)
Have some wine.

Alice looks all round the table. There is nothing on it but tea.

ALICE
I don't see any wine.

MARCH HARE
There isn't any.

ALICE
Then it wasn't very civil of you to offer it.

MARCH HARE
It wasn't very civil of you to sit down without being invited.

The Hatter has been studying Alice for some time, head on one side.

HATTER
Your hair wants cutting.

ALICE
You should learn not to make personal remarks. It's very rude.

The Hatter opens his eyes very wide.

HATTER
Why is a raven like a writing-desk?

ALICE
(to herself, pleased)
Come, we shall have some fun now. I'm glad they've begun asking riddles.
(aloud)
I believe I can guess that.

MARCH HARE
Do you mean that you think you can find out the answer to it?

ALICE
Exactly so.

MARCH HARE
Then you should say what you mean.

ALICE
(hastily)
I do. At least, I mean what I say. That's the same thing, you know.

HATTER
Not the same thing a bit! You might just as well say that "I see what I eat" is the same thing as "I eat what I see"!

MARCH HARE
You might just as well say that "I like what I get" is the same thing as "I get what I like"!

DORMOUSE
(talking in its sleep)
You might just as well say that "I breathe when I sleep" is the same thing as "I sleep when I breathe"!

HATTER
It *is* the same thing with you.

The conversation stops. Nobody says anything for a minute.

The Hatter takes a WATCH out of his pocket, shakes it, holds it to his ear, and looks at it uneasily.

HATTER (CONT'D)
What day of the month is it?

ALICE
(after a moment)
The fourth.

HATTER
(sighing)
Two days wrong!
(glaring at the Hare)
I told you butter wouldn't suit the works!

MARCH HARE
(meekly)
It was the *best* butter.

HATTER
Yes, but some crumbs must have got in as well. You shouldn't have put it in with the bread-knife.

The March Hare takes the watch, looks at it gloomily, dips it into his cup of tea, and looks at it again.

MARCH HARE
It was the *best* butter, you know.

Alice leans over his shoulder.

ALICE
What a funny watch! It tells the day of the month, and doesn't tell what o'clock it is!

HATTER
Why should it? Does *your* watch tell you what year it is?

ALICE
(readily)
Of course not. But that's because it stays the same year for such a long time together.

HATTER
Which is just the case with *mine*.

Alice stares at him, thoroughly puzzled.

HATTER (CONT'D)
(to the Hare)
The Dormouse is asleep again.

He pours a little hot tea on the Dormouse's nose. It shakes its head impatiently, eyes still shut.

DORMOUSE
Of course, of course; just what I was going to remark myself.

HATTER
(turning to Alice)
Have you guessed the riddle yet?

ALICE
No, I give it up. What's the answer?

HATTER
I haven't the slightest idea.

MARCH HARE
Nor I.

Alice sighs wearily.

ALICE
I think you might do something better with the time than wasting it in asking riddles that have no answers.

HATTER
If you knew Time as well as I do, you wouldn't talk about wasting *it*. It's *him*.

ALICE
I don't know what you mean.

HATTER
(tossing his head contemptuously)
Of course you don't! I dare say you never even spoke to Time!

ALICE
(cautiously)
Perhaps not. But I know I have to beat time when I learn music.

HATTER
Ah! That accounts for it. He won't stand beating.

He leans across the table, confidential.

HATTER (CONT'D)
We quarrelled last March, just before *he* went mad, you know.

He points his teaspoon at the March Hare, who goes on dipping the watch.

HATTER (CONT'D)
And ever since that, he won't do a thing I ask! It's always six o'clock now.

Alice looks along the table at all the cups. A bright idea.

ALICE
Is that the reason so many tea-things are put out here?

HATTER
(with a sigh)
Yes, that's it. It's always tea-time, and we've no time to wash the things between whiles.

ALICE
Then you keep moving round, I suppose?

HATTER
Exactly so. As the things get used up.

ALICE
But what happens when you come to the beginning again?

MARCH HARE
(yawning)
Suppose we change the subject.

HATTER
I want a clean cup. Let's all move one place on.

He moves on as he speaks. The Dormouse follows him. The March Hare moves into the Dormouse's place, and Alice, rather unwillingly, takes the March Hare's.

The March Hare has just upset the milk-jug into his plate. Alice's new place is much worse than her old one.

She has had enough. She gets up and walks off through the trees.

Behind her, neither of them takes the least notice. As she looks back, they are trying to put the Dormouse into the teapot.

ALICE
(to herself)
At any rate I'll never go *there* again! It's the stupidest tea-party I ever was at in all my life!

> CUT TO:

INT. THE LONG HALL - CONTINUOUS

Alice steps through a door in a tree trunk and finds herself back in the long hall, close to the little glass table.

She takes the little golden key and unlocks the door that leads into the garden.

> FADE OUT.
